==Old English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈseːon/|lang=ang}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{proto|Germanic|sehwanan|lang=ang}}.  Cognate with Old Frisian ''sīa'' (West Frisian ''[[sjen]]''), Old High German ''[[sehan]]'' (German ''[[sehen]]''), Old Saxon ''[[sehan]]'', Dutch ''[[zien]]'', Old Norse {{term|sjá|lang=non}} (Danish and Swedish {{term|se|lang=se}}, Icelandic {{term|sjá|lang=is}}), Gothic [[𐍃𐌰𐌹𐍈𐌰𐌽]].

====Verb====
{{ang-verb|head=sēon|type=strong|class=V}}

# to [[see]]

=====Conjugation=====
{{ang-conj|type=strong|V|sēo|siehst|siehþ|sēoþ|sēo|sēon|seah|sāwe, sǣġe|seah|sāwon, sǣgon|sāwe, sǣġe|sāwen, sǣġen|seoh|sēoþ|sēonde|(ġe)sewen, (ġe)seġen}}

=====Descendants=====
* English: [[see]]

===Etymology 2===
{{term|seon|sēon|to be|lang=ang}}. Akin to {{etyl|goh|-}} ''[[sin|sīn]]'' "to be", Latin ''[[sum]]'' "to be", Old English ''[[soþ|sōþ]]'' "true, sooth"

====Verb====
{{ang-verb|type=irreg|head=sēon}}

# To [[be]], used primarily in reference to God
#: '' Fæder ūser, þū sē '''eart''' on heofonum, '''sī''' þīn nama gehālgod '' &ndash; Our Father, who art in heaven, hallowed be thy name.

=====Conjugation=====
{{ang-conj|type=irreg||eom|(eart)|is|sind(on), sint · (earon)|sīe|sīen|(wæs)|(wǣre)|(wæs)|(wǣron)|(wǣre)|(wǣren)|sī, sēo|''unattested''|(wesende)|''unattested''}}

===Etymology 3===
From {{proto|Germanic|sīganan||sīhwanan|to strain, drip|lang=ang}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|seyk-|to pour, strain|lang=ang}}.

====Verb====
{{ang-verb|head=sēon|type=strong|class=I}}

# {{transitive}} to [[strain]], [[filter]]
# {{intransitive}} to [[run]] as a sore, ooze, [[trickle]], [[drop]], [[drip]]

